<div class="row">
    <div class="small-12 columns">
        <h1>Junior School</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="small-12 island">
        <h2 class="subheader">STAR and PAT Tests</h2>

        <section class="content">
            <p><strong>STAR Test:</strong> There are four parts to the STAR Test which assess different reading skills including Word Recognition, Sentence Comprehension, Paragraph Comprehension and Vocabulary Range.</p>
            <p><strong>PAT Tests:</strong> Progressive Achievement Tests are a series of standardised tests developed specifically for New Zealand Schools. There are four different test: Reading Comprehension, Reading Vocabulary, Listening Comprehension and Mathematics.</p>

            <div class="row">
                <div class="small-12 medium-6 columns">
                    <img src="/custom/diocesan/achievementreport/img/comingsoon/01.gif" />
                </div>
                <div class="small-12 medium-6 columns">
                    <img src="/custom/diocesan/achievementreport/img/comingsoon/02.gif" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>

    <div class="small-12 island">
        <h2 class="subheader">Reading</h2>
        <section class="content">
            <p>Achievement and progress in reading is assessed using a variety of tools. Two of the common tools, Running Records and PROBE reading tests are used in the Junior School. In a running record it is the level number that indicates achievement and progress. The 25 levels used in running records are divided into colour bands. A PROBE reading test returns a reading age based on a detailed analysis of your daughter's responses to a text. It is not unusual to notice a slight dip in achievement as the students move from being assessed using running records to being assessed using a PROBE test.</p>

            <img src="/custom/diocesan/achievementreport/img/comingsoon/03.gif" />
        </section>
    </div>

    <div class="small-12 island">
        <h2 class="subheader">Writing</h2>
        <section class="content">
            <p>Achievement and progress in writing is assessed using both e-asTTle an online assessment tool designed to assess your daughter's progress in writing from Years 1&ndash;6 and overall teacher judgement. When assessing achievement and progress a series of levels are used. These levels go from 1 to 6and are further broken down into three stages: beginning, proficient and advanced. In order the levels are 1B, 1P, 1A: 2B, 2P, 2A etc.</p>
            <p>Through this process teachers develop a rich picture of your daughter's strengths and next learning steps through analysing information from learning conversations, observations of learning tasks or the products of these tasks, and the use of the standardised assessment tool e-asTTle. These methods of assessment can be thought of as a 'healthy triangle', with standardised tools (such as e-asTTle) being only a small part of the picture.</p>

            <img src="/custom/diocesan/achievementreport/img/comingsoon/04.gif" />
        </section>
    </div>

    <div class="small-12 island">
        <h2 class="subheader">Mathematics</h2>
        <section class="content">
            <p>Achievement and progress in mathematics is assessed using a variety of tools. PR1ME mathematics advocates using a range of assessment strategies including Overall Teacher Judgment, chapter tests and analysis of the data that assesses student's knowledge of number and problem solving abilities. One on one conferencing during maths group teaching also provides teachers with rich information about your daughter's mathematical thinking, as well as identifying strengths, needs and next steps for teaching and learning. This time also assists teachers with grouping, identifying trends, planning, and transferring information between classes and schools.</p>

            <img src="/custom/diocesan/achievementreport/img/comingsoon/05.gif" />
        </section>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row">
    <div class="small-12 columns">
        <h1>Junior High</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="small-12 island">
        <h2 class="subheader">PAT Tests</h2>

        <section class="content">
            <p><strong>PAT: Listening Comprehension</strong> is a test of assessing students' comprehension of texts read to them. The test is made up of a number of short texts (for instance, narratives, poems and informational texts) each of which is followed by a series of questions. Students listen to each text and show their understanding by answering the questions.</p>
            <p><strong>PAT: Mathematics</strong> is a test of mathematics. The questions are chosen to assess the skills and knowledge described in the New Zealand mathematics curriculum. Students answer questions involving number knowledge, number strategies, algebra, geometry and measurement, and statistics.</p>
            <p><strong>PAT: Reading Comprehension</strong> is a test of how well students understand what they read. The test is made up of a number of short texts (for instance, stories, explanations, descriptions and poems) each of which is followed by a series of questions. Students read each text and show their understanding by answering the questions.</p>
            <p><strong>PAT: Reading Vocabulary</strong> is a test of how well students understand the words that they read. Each question in the test is made up of a sentence containing a word printed in bold. Students must choose a word from a list of options that has the same or nearly the same meaning as the bolded word. Strong vocabulary knowledge is associated with high levels of reading achievement.</p>

            <img src="/custom/diocesan/achievementreport/img/comingsoon/06.gif" />
        </section>
    </div>
</div>
